\[ E = \frac{hc}{\lambda} \]

Let's calculate the energies for both wavelengths:

1. For wavelength \( \lambda_1 = 400 \, \text{nm} = 400 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m} \):

\[
E_1 = \frac{(6.63 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J·s}) \cdot (3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s})}{400 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m}} = \frac{(6.63 \times 3) \times 10^{-26}}{400} = \frac{19.89 \times 10^{-26}}{400} \approx 4.9725 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}
\]

Converting this to electron volts \( (1 \, \text{eV} = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}) \):

\[
E_1 \approx \frac{4.9725 \times 10^{-19}}{1.60 \times 10^{-19}} \approx 3.105 \, \text{eV}
\]

2. For wavelength \( \lambda_2 = 300 \, \text{nm} = 300 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m} \):

\[
E_2 = \frac{(6.63 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J·s}) \cdot (3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s})}{300 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m}} \approx \frac{(6.63 \times 3) \times 10^{-26}}{300} = \frac{19.89 \times 10^{-26}}{300} \approx 6.63 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}
\}

Again, converting to electron volts:

\[
E_2 \approx \frac{6.63 \times 10^{-19}}{1.60 \times 10^{-19}} \approx 4.144 \, \text{eV}
\]

3. Now, to find the change in stopping voltage under these two scenarios, we we can consider that the stopping potential \( V_s \) is equal to the energy of the emitted electron in eV. 

The difference in stopping voltages:

\[
\Delta V = V_{s,2} - V_{s,1} = E_2 - E_1 \approx 4.144 \, \text{eV} - 3.105 \, \text{eV} \approx 1.039 \, \text{eV}
\]

Thus, it increase by approximately 1.04 V.

